---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_NOTE, SITE } from "../lib/site.mjs";

const description =
  "Draft community guidelines for XHere, including the category rules and the stricter rules for incident reports.";
---

<Base title="Community guidelines" description={description} draft>
  <h1>Community guidelines</h1>
  <p>
    XHere is for information about places, not about hunting people.
    These rules match the category rules stored with the app.
    This page is a draft for {SITE.owner} to review. It is not in effect until he says so.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Describe the place and what happened. Do not describe a person.</li>
    <li>Be accurate. Say what you saw, and do not dress up a guess as a fact.</li>
    <li>Keep phone numbers and email addresses out of the text. Contact stays in the app.</li>
    <li>In an emergency, call 911. XHere is not a reporting channel to police.</li>
    <li>Do not sell or give away weapons, drugs, or other regulated items.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Everyone</h2>
  <p>These are not allowed on any drop, comment, or profile:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Harassment, threats, or hate toward a person or a group.</li>
    <li>Sexual content involving anyone under 18.</li>
    <li>Private contact details, documents, or account information.</li>
    <li>Impersonation, spam, and ads that have nothing to do with the place.</li>
    <li>Naming or describing a suspect, a witness, or a neighbor so that someone could pick them out of a crowd.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Report, block, and hide</h2>
  <p>
    Open any drop to report it, report its author, block its author, or hide just that drop for yourself.
    In the app these are on the drop screen. Settings › Community guidelines has this page, and Settings › Blocked and hidden lets you undo a block or a hide.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Report.</strong> Pick a reason and add details if you like. Reports are private. The author is not told who reported.</li>
    <li><strong>Block.</strong> Blocking works both ways: you won’t see their drops, and they won’t see yours. They are not told.</li>
    <li><strong>Hide for me.</strong> Hides one drop from you only. Nobody else is affected, and the author is not told.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatic hiding.</strong> Two reports hide a hazard, safety alert, or incident report until a moderator looks. Other categories hide after the number of reports set on that category, usually three.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>History and stories</h2>
  <p>
    Historical facts, stories, and memorials reveal within about 150 meters and can stay up.
    A source or citation is encouraged. Three nearby confirmations earn the confirmed badge.
  </p>

  <h2>Nature</h2>
  <p>
    Viewpoints, trail notes, and water sources can stay up and reveal within about 300 meters.
    A wildlife sighting expires after 7 days so an old animal report does not sit on the map forever.
  </p>

  <h2>Food and events</h2>
  <p>
    A food truck lasts 6 hours. Someone nearby can say “still here,” which renews it. Other food tips can stay up.
    An event or pop-up needs a start and an end, and it expires when it ends.
    A new account can only post an event that ends within 30 days.
  </p>

  <h2>Sales and free stuff</h2>
  <p>
    Yard sales last 3 days. Items for sale and free stuff last 14 days.
    The author is always shown. Anonymous sales are not allowed.
    Contact is in the app only. The text filter removes phone numbers and email addresses.
    No weapons, drugs, or regulated items. The posting screen says this before you publish.
  </p>

  <h2>Hazards and safety alerts</h2>
  <p>
    A hazard (road, sidewalk, flood) reveals from farther away, about 2 kilometers, and lasts 24 hours unless people nearby confirm it.
    Each confirmation can extend it by 24 hours, up to 72 hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    A safety alert is sensitive. It lasts 72 hours. New accounts are reviewed before it is visible.
    The same emergency line applies: call 911 if you need help. XHere is not a way to reach the police.
    Describe what happened, not who.
  </p>

  <h2>Incident reports</h2>
  <p>
    An incident report is for a crime or similar event nearby. It is the strictest category, because a location app can do real harm here.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who can post.</strong> Regular accounts and above. A new account cannot post one.</li>
    <li><strong>Where.</strong> Only where a local police tip line is set up. For now that is the Jamestown area, where the app shows the Jamestown Police anonymous tip line.</li>
    <li><strong>Review.</strong> Posts are held for review first. Trusted accounts can go live, still marked unconfirmed.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirmation.</strong> It stays unconfirmed until two people nearby say it is still true. Two denials, or two reports, hide it.</li>
    <li><strong>Place.</strong> The map shows an approximate area of about 100 meters, not the exact spot and not a person’s home as a pin.</li>
    <li><strong>Time.</strong> It expires after 72 hours. You have to say when it happened.</li>
    <li><strong>Words.</strong> Describe what happened, not who. Do not name or describe people. The filter blocks contact details, house numbers with a street name, and words for gender, age, race, clothing, build, and similar descriptors.</li>
    <li><strong>Acknowledgement.</strong> The app asks you to accept these rules before the post goes through.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency.</strong> In an emergency call 911. XHere is not a reporting channel to police.</li>
    <li><strong>The website.</strong> A share link shows the teaser only. It does not include the hidden text, the author’s handle, or an exact address.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where a city publishes an official incident feed, that layer is separate and labeled as open data.
    User reports are not a substitute for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Have information about a crime? Jamestown Police anonymous tip line
    <a href="tel:+17164838477">(716) 483-8477</a> (<a href="https://www.jamestownny.gov/police-department/anonymous-tip-line/">jamestownny.gov</a>).
    Non-emergency <a href="tel:+17164837536">(716) 483-7536</a>. Emergency 911.
  </p>

  <h2>Community, infrastructure, secrets, art, notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Places of worship and community spaces can stay up. A notice lasts 30 days and needs a Regular account.</li>
    <li>Infrastructure and underground notes reveal within about 200 meters. Construction expires when the estimated completion date passes.</li>
    <li>Hidden gems and local tips reveal only within about 50 meters. You have to be there.</li>
    <li>Street art, public art, and music spots reveal within about 150 meters.</li>
    <li>A general note reveals within about 100 meters and lasts 7 days, unless two confirmations keep it. New accounts are reviewed first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If we remove something</h2>
  <p>
    Moderators can approve a held drop, hide or remove a drop, and suspend an account. Every action, and the reason, is kept in an audit log that cannot be edited or deleted.
    A formal appeals screen is not built yet. Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> if you think a decision was wrong. {CONTACT_NOTE}
  </p>
</Base>
